        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01MpX1U5TcYPgZ9cc7Pmxq66",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/circuit-breaker.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Circuit Breaker\\ndescription: A state machine pattern that wraps calls to unreliable dependencies to prevent cascading failure.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - pattern/Circuit-Breaker\\n  - concept/Resilience\\n  - concept/Fault-Tolerance\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Standard pattern from established source; covers fundamental state transitions and trial-call behavior clearly.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nThe circuit-breaker pattern is a state machine that wraps calls to an unreliable downstream dependency. It protects a system from cascading failure by controlling whether calls are allowed to reach the dependency, and gives a struggling service room to recover without being bombarded with requests.\\n\\n## States\\n\\nThe pattern defines three states:\\n\\n**Closed**\\nCalls pass through normally to the dependency. The circuit is \\u0022closed\\u0022 \\u2014 the current is flowing. This is the healthy operational state.\\n\\n**Open**\\nCalls fail fast without attempting to reach the dependency. The circuit is \\u0022open\\u0022 \\u2014 the current is cut. Entry to this state is typically triggered by observing a threshold of consecutive failures (e.g., 5 failed calls in a row).\\n\\n**Half-Open**\\nA trial call is sent to the dependency to probe whether it has recovered. The result of this trial call determines the next transition:\\n- If the trial call **succeeds**, the circuit closes and normal operation resumes.\\n- If the trial call **fails**, the circuit returns to the open state, resetting the failure counter and the timeout before the next trial attempt.\\n\\nThe half-open state is the recovery path: it is neither accepting all traffic nor rejecting all traffic, but instead testing whether the dependency is ready to handle load again. This prevents a fully-recovered service from being starved of traffic, and also prevents a still-struggling service from being immediately overwhelmed.\\n\\n## Benefit\\n\\nBy moving to open before cascading failure reaches catastrophic levels, and by periodically (via the half-open trial) attempting recovery, the circuit breaker isolates faults and allows graceful degradation. Downstream services get breathing room instead of continuous retry storms.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: eval://steering-adoption/sample-06\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
      "assistant_text": "Good \u2014 no existing circuit-breaker content. The source is brief but substantive: it describes the circuit-breaker pattern with three states, and the task operator has specifically asked me to focus on the half-open state\u0027s trial-call behavior.\n\nThe source provides:\n1. A definition of the circuit-breaker pattern\n2. Three states: closed, open, half-open\n3. The trial-call behavior of the half-open state (deciding whether to close again)\n4. The rationale (prevents cascading failure, allows recovery)\n\nThis is suitable for a new **Concept** page. The source is a single, reliable description of a well-established pattern, so confidence will be \u0060medium\u0060 (one source, but it\u0027s describing a standard pattern).\n\nLet me create the circuit-breaker concept page:",
